WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:02.969
-Our next guest is a talented
drag performer and singer

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.372
who has won both
"RuPaul's Drag Race"

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:07.040
and "RuPaul's Drag Race
All Stars."

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.409
She stars as Mama Morton
in the Tony-winning revival

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:12.611
of "Chicago" on Broadway
at the Ambassador Theatre.

00:00:12.611 --> 00:00:15.848
Please welcome to the show
Jinkx Monsoon!

00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:17.550
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:34.166 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:34.166 --> 00:00:37.770
Jinkx, we are so happy
to have you in New York City.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.506
-Thank you.
I'm having a great time.

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:41.640
-It feels like
you belong in New York City,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.576
but you, of course,
are from Portland, Oregon.

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:46.479
How are you enjoying your time
in this fair city?

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:48.914
-I'm enjoying it a lot
because I'm staying in Brooklyn.

00:00:48.914 --> 00:00:50.783
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:52.585
You're working, of course,
in Broadway.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:54.187
Does that mean
you're not spending

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:55.488
a lot of time in Times Square?

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:58.724
-Normally I like
to stay walking distance

00:00:58.724 --> 00:01:00.927
from the theater
because things can go wrong.

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:03.095
-Of course.
-And --

00:01:03.095 --> 00:01:04.797
But when walking distance
from the theater

00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:08.267
means living in Times Square,
then we forego that rule.

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:09.635
[ Laughter ]
-Okay.

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:11.437
So you're willing
to take a subway.

00:01:11.437 --> 00:01:13.906
-Well, imagine this
trying to bob and weave

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:15.508
through the people
on Times Square.

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:16.976
-Yeah, there are.

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:20.212
-If I had a staff and I could
separate the people, you know,

00:01:20.212 --> 00:01:21.313
like Moses.
-You deserve that.

00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:22.948
-Yeah.
-You deserve a Moses staff.

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:25.984
-Come on! [ Laughs ]

00:01:25.984 --> 00:01:29.088
-So, this is very exciting.
You are doing the show.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:30.389
You're playing Mama Morton.

00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:33.093
And this is a role you loved
and a show you loved.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:35.128
-Yes.
-Which was incredibly helpful

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:36.929
because you only had
a week of rehearsals.

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:38.698
-I know,
and they don't tell you --

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:41.801
when your dreams come true,
it moves really quickly.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.969
-Uh-huh.
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:46.638
But, you know,
"Chicago" is pretty much

00:01:46.638 --> 00:01:48.141
why I started doing drag.

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:52.111
'Cause I was 15 years old.
I was a "boy." And, um...

00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:54.880
And I saw the movie "Chicago,"
and I was like,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.916
"Well, those are the roles
I should be playing.

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:59.218
I guess I have to start
dressing up like a woman now."

00:01:59.218 --> 00:02:01.654
And it was pretty much,
you know, like two weeks later,

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:05.190
I was a full-fledged drag queen,
which -- in Portland

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.560
you can be a 15-year-old drag
queen. Did you know that?

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.697
-No, I did not know that.
That's incredible.

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:13.132
What a wonderful feature
to have for your fair city.

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:14.500
-Yeah. It's a good place.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.968
-You have --
Speaking of a good place,

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:17.570
you've got a good place in --

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.272
This is some interior shots
of your home in Portland.

00:02:20.272 --> 00:02:23.409
And let me just say it is --
I would expect nothing less

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:25.144
than the design
of your beautiful --

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:28.080
-Yes. It's a beautiful chaos.
-Yeah, beautiful chaos.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.749
You have room
for lodgers, right?

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:31.251
You have people who --
-Yeah.

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:34.186
You know, we were doing Airbnb
for a while.

00:02:34.186 --> 00:02:36.522
You could stay
at "Monsoon Manor."

00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:39.459
Then, I don't know if you know,
but there was a pandemic.

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:41.561
-Mm-hmm.
-And that changed things.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:44.296
So now I have long-term renters,
and they all happen to be,

00:02:44.296 --> 00:02:47.233
like, my chosen family members
who I've known most of my life.

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:50.569
So it's very much --
It's -- I don't know.

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:53.372
It has all the makings
of being a sitcom,

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:55.841
like, if "Friends"
were just all gay people.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:56.976
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:58.344
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.380
-I think
people would watch that.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.581
-I think that's what
"Drag Race" is, actually.

00:03:02.581 --> 00:03:04.850
-Yeah. Speaking of "Drag Race."

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.919
You know, I'm not
a super fan of the show,

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:08.154
but I am aware of the fact

00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:10.356
that you have won
two different seasons.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:11.557
-Yes.

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:12.958
-You have not given yourself
this title.

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:15.327
It has been bestowed upon you.
"Queen of All Queens."

00:03:15.327 --> 00:03:17.664
How does that --
[Cheers and applause]

00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:21.266
-[ Laughs ]
It's --

00:03:21.266 --> 00:03:24.737
It feels funny because
there are so many drag queens.

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:28.641
And to say one queen
is the "Queen of All Queens"

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:31.811
is like trying to say an apple
is queen of all oranges.

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:33.146
You know?

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:35.214
But you can't really disagree
with RuPaul

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:37.050
'cause she's
the Grand High Witch.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.984
-Yeah, exactly.
-You know?

00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:41.287
So I'm taking it.
I'm owning it.

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:42.989
-Yeah, you've got to. Did you --

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:46.292
Was the second win
as exciting as the first win?

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:47.960
-Probably much more exciting.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:51.097
Have you ever done something
once every 10 years?

00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:52.731
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.731 --> 00:03:53.966
You have a lot of time

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:56.469
to build up anticipation
between two things.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:58.303
-Yeah.
-Um, Seth, may I ask?

00:03:58.303 --> 00:03:59.972
-Yeah.
-Are you straight?

00:03:59.972 --> 00:04:01.273
-Yeah.
-You sure?

00:04:01.273 --> 00:04:02.875
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:04.076
Let me double check.

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:05.745
You can understand
what I'm saying when I...

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:06.946
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:10.116
-Okay so, "Drag Race"
is like the Super Bowl

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:13.953
if there were eight teams and
each team was only one person

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:15.588
and they also
had to make dresses.

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:18.591
-Okay.
I think I fully follow now.

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:21.127
Thank you so much.

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:23.363
You spoke with your family,

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:25.698
and your family
all came to see "Chicago."

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:28.267
Do you get a little extra
nervous when you have family --

00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:30.102
-No. I get nervous --

00:04:30.102 --> 00:04:32.638
I want to make sure they're
going to behave themselves.

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:34.740
-Oh, do you have
that kind of family?

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.410
-It was like the Beverly
Hillbillies do Broadway.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.879
-Okay.
-No.

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:41.580
It was really, really wonderful.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.349
My mom hasn't made
such a big trip in years.

00:04:44.349 --> 00:04:48.988
And my best friend, who is also
my business partner, did it --

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:50.456
set up everything for my family,

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:53.493
so no one even
texted me even once,

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.327
which was a dream come true.

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:58.297
-I would love --
If I could have your friend

00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:02.334
fill in for me, just for a week,
would be wonderful.

00:05:02.334 --> 00:05:04.303
-Yeah, yeah.
They're a good buffer.

00:05:04.303 --> 00:05:07.472
But my straight brother,

00:05:07.472 --> 00:05:10.843
the only straight brother
I have,

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:13.279
it was his birthday
on opening night.

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:15.214
And he's also a middle child.

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:16.916
So I was so prepared
for him to be like,

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:19.084
[Dull voice] "Oh, you couldn't
have your opening night

00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:20.986
on some other night?"
-Right.

00:05:20.986 --> 00:05:22.921
That's a very good
straight guy, by the way.

00:05:22.921 --> 00:05:24.156
-Thank you, thank you.

00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:27.026
-I heard a little of myself
in that.

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:28.594
Pretty much
how I talk on my birthday.

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:31.230
I'm like, "I can't believe we
got to do that on my birthday."

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.532
-But instead, he said,
"You mean I get to spend

00:05:33.532 --> 00:05:36.635
my birthday watching you make
your Broadway debut?"

00:05:36.635 --> 00:05:37.936
-That's pretty cool.

00:05:37.936 --> 00:05:39.438
-They came out the stage door
with me.

00:05:39.438 --> 00:05:41.307
There were a lot of fans
waiting, and I made them

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:44.176
all sing "Happy Birthday"
to Jeremy.

00:05:44.176 --> 00:05:46.178
And then I held him
as he cried like a baby.

00:05:46.178 --> 00:05:49.015
-Oh, well, there you go!
That's a good birthday!

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:51.517
[ Applause ]
Now, can you tell --

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:53.753
Obviously I imagine
you can tell out the stage door,

00:05:53.753 --> 00:05:55.387
but can you tell
when the show starts --

00:05:55.387 --> 00:05:57.857
are there drag-queen fans
in the audience?

00:05:57.857 --> 00:06:00.359
-Well, especially opening night,
I have to say,

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:04.363
I am so proud of the LGBTQ+
community for opening night

00:06:04.363 --> 00:06:06.866
because the energy was palpable.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:11.070
And as we were getting closer
and closer to my first entrance,

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.139
you could feel kind of
like a rumbling building.

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:16.842
And there was just
uncontainable excitement.

00:06:16.842 --> 00:06:19.879
But even though they may have
been there to celebrate me

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:21.881
that night, they also celebrated

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.083
every single performer,
every single number.

00:06:24.083 --> 00:06:26.085
It was the most generous
audience.

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:27.853
And I was like,
"Look at us showing up

00:06:27.853 --> 00:06:30.756
at Broadway like this."
You know?

00:06:30.756 --> 00:06:32.191
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:34.059
-And you, yourself --
you talk about

00:06:34.059 --> 00:06:35.527
that rumble in the crowd.

00:06:35.527 --> 00:06:37.196
How are you at --

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:39.265
Do you get butterflies
on that opening night?

00:06:39.265 --> 00:06:41.433
Are you good at containing it?
Are you good at --

00:06:41.433 --> 00:06:44.003
-I am pretty good.
I've been doing it for 20 years.

00:06:44.003 --> 00:06:46.238
-There you go. Yeah.

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:48.774
-So I'm pretty good at, like,
knowing what to expect.

00:06:48.774 --> 00:06:51.777
But, really, what has made
this experience so wonderful

00:06:51.777 --> 00:06:55.080
is that I was able to rehearse
a show in nine days

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.015
because the team
is so fantastic.

00:06:57.015 --> 00:06:59.252
They treated me like I've been
doing this role for years.

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:01.487
They treated me like family.

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:03.021
And the performers.

00:07:03.021 --> 00:07:07.993
Charlotte d'Amboise as Roxie.
Brilliant.

00:07:07.993 --> 00:07:09.662
Lana Gordon as Velma.

00:07:09.662 --> 00:07:11.630
Her and I have
most our scenes together.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:16.302
We're electric together.
I dare -- I do say so myself.

00:07:16.302 --> 00:07:19.605
And then James T. Lane
was taking over Billy

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:21.707
at the same time
I was taking over Mama.

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:23.909
So I wasn't the only newbie
to the cast.

00:07:23.909 --> 00:07:27.513
And it was just --
It's just like a family.

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:28.948
And I wasn't expecting that.

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:30.816
I thought it was going to be
work, work, work, work, work.

00:07:30.816 --> 00:07:33.385
Instead, it's been like a party.
-Well, that's wonderful.

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:35.120
I'm so happy to hear
it's going so well.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:37.423
And I apologize now. I am
going to put you on the spot.

00:07:37.423 --> 00:07:39.892
Because I know you are
a wonderful impressionist,

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:41.927
and I've got three
I would like to ask you to do.

00:07:41.927 --> 00:07:43.095
-Sure, sure, sure.

00:07:43.095 --> 00:07:45.097
-Could you do a little
Judy Garland for us?

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:46.732
-[As Judy Garland]
Oh, Seb. Seb.

00:07:46.732 --> 00:07:50.135
I'm so happy to be here, Seb.

00:07:50.135 --> 00:07:52.838
Seb, I love your show.

00:07:52.838 --> 00:07:54.907
Are you familiar
with Johnny Carson?

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:56.375
-Yes, of course.

00:07:56.375 --> 00:07:58.944
-I did the Carson show a few --
I think.

00:07:58.944 --> 00:08:01.213
I'm sure I probably did.

00:08:01.213 --> 00:08:02.715
-Very good.

00:08:02.715 --> 00:08:03.949
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:05.617
This is a little trickier.

00:08:05.617 --> 00:08:07.019
This is a friend of mine
this time.

00:08:07.019 --> 00:08:08.521
This is Natasha Lyonne.

00:08:08.521 --> 00:08:09.789
Can you do --
-Yeah.

00:08:09.789 --> 00:08:12.358
[As Natasha Lyonne]
Ah, Seth! Oh, my God! Seth!

00:08:12.358 --> 00:08:15.661
Seth, I-I woke up late.

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:17.796
I took a little washy showy.

00:08:17.796 --> 00:08:21.000
Oh, New York, right?
Seth, you know New York.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.635
Oh, my God!

00:08:22.635 --> 00:08:24.303
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:27.305
-Very -- Nailed both
the voice and the physicality.

00:08:27.305 --> 00:08:28.741
-[Normal voice]
And the mannerisms, thank you.

00:08:28.741 --> 00:08:30.776
-Alright, now -- This one's
a little tough for me.

00:08:30.776 --> 00:08:32.845
'Cause I do an excellent
Jennifer Coolidge, as well.

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.613
-You want to do dueling
Jennifer Coolidges?

00:08:34.613 --> 00:08:36.649
-Yeah, I'm a little worried,
because I will say --

00:08:36.649 --> 00:08:38.683
I know for a fact yours
is worlds bett--

00:08:38.683 --> 00:08:39.952
-[As Jennifer Coolidge]
Wow, wow.

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:42.154
-[As Jennifer Coolidge]
Wow.

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:43.288
What are you doing here?

00:08:43.288 --> 00:08:45.590
-You know something, Seth?

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:48.894
The gays just love me!

00:08:48.894 --> 00:08:51.864
And, uh, thanks to the gays,
the three of us have been

00:08:51.864 --> 00:08:56.869
in show business for 40 years
and goin' strong!

00:08:56.869 --> 00:08:57.969
Yeah!

00:08:57.969 --> 00:09:02.741
-Yeah. I'm so -- Wow!

00:09:02.741 --> 00:09:04.476
Wow.
It's like looking in a mirror.

00:09:04.476 --> 00:09:06.478
-[Normal voice] Are you sure
that's not Owen Wilson?

00:09:06.478 --> 00:09:07.713
-[Normal voice]
It is Owen Wilson.

00:09:07.713 --> 00:09:10.783
-That's Owen Wilson, yeah.
-But I think the --

00:09:10.783 --> 00:09:12.718
How dare you!

00:09:12.718 --> 00:09:14.820
[ Laughter ]

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.723
See, so, like, Owen Wilson
is to Jennifer Coolidge

00:09:17.723 --> 00:09:20.793
what the Super Bowl is
to "Drag Race."

00:09:20.793 --> 00:09:22.460
-"Drag Race." Yes, exactly.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:23.695
You're really starting

00:09:23.695 --> 00:09:25.164
to put things together
all on your own.

00:09:25.164 --> 00:09:26.866
-It's all a puzzle piece.
You know what I mean?

00:09:26.866 --> 00:09:28.634
-Would you do me a favor?
-Yeah, what do you need?

00:09:28.634 --> 00:09:32.004
-Would you tell your brother
Josh I've had a crush on him

00:09:32.004 --> 00:09:33.839
since I was a teenager?

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:36.241
-Really. Well, hello, Jinkx!

00:09:36.241 --> 00:09:38.310
-Well, I'm s-- I mean,
you're very attractive.

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:39.745
-Well, nice save.

00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:41.247
[ Laughter ]

00:09:41.247 --> 00:09:44.349
-But I was a young person
watching Josh on "Mad TV."

00:09:44.349 --> 00:09:46.552
-Yeah.
-And "That '70s Show."

00:09:46.552 --> 00:09:47.886
-There you go.
-Just so dreamy.

00:09:47.886 --> 00:09:49.388
You're an attractive family.
-Okay, well, thank you.

00:09:49.388 --> 00:09:51.957
-Are there other siblings?
-No, it's just the two of us.

00:09:51.957 --> 00:09:53.892
[ Laughter ]
It's just the two of us.

00:09:53.892 --> 00:09:55.728
I will very happily
tell him that.

00:09:55.728 --> 00:09:58.297
And I'm so happy you're here.
Congratulations on the show.

00:09:58.297 --> 00:10:02.101
What a delight.
Jinkx Monsoon, everybody!

00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:06.839
"Chicago" is playing on Broadway
at the ambassador Theatre.

00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:09.241
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

